cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "aperturediv: multi-aperture receive diversity for free-space optical links"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-06
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Receive diversity for a scintillating free-space optical link, with the
  inter-aperture correlation treated as a first-class input rather than
  assumed away. Unit-mean lognormal and gamma-gamma irradiance statistics
  with the scintillation index checked against each model's own closed form;
  the aperture-averaging factor computed from its defining integral over the
  circular-aperture overlap kernel, with two closed-form limits derived and
  used as validation targets; the exact lognormal relation between
  log-irradiance and irradiance correlation, and Gaussian-copula samplers for
  both channel models; maximal-ratio, equal-gain and selection combining with
  outage probability, finite-window diversity order and the measured cost of
  the independent-aperture idealisation; and a learned combiner for imperfect
  or stale channel-state information, benchmarked against maximal-ratio
  combining with the true state (optimal by Cauchy-Schwarz, so an upper bound
  no model can pass), maximal-ratio combining with the estimate, equal-gain
  combining, and a one-parameter analytic shrinkage family, with a
  penalty-quantile uncertainty output. Validation level 2, research grade.
  Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace
  use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - free-space-optics
  - scintillation
  - aperture-averaging
  - diversity-combining
  - maximal-ratio-combining
  - gamma-gamma
  - lognormal-fading
  - outage-probability
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Laser Beam Propagation through Random Media"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "SPIE Press"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Standard reference for optical scintillation, the lognormal and
      gamma-gamma irradiance models, and aperture averaging. This repository
      does not quote a page number or a tabulated constant from it: the
      aperture-averaging factor is computed from its defining integral and the
      two closed-form limits used as validation targets are derived in the
      source docstrings of aperturediv.aperture.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Al-Habash
        given-names: "M. A."
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: >-
      Mathematical model for the irradiance probability density function of a
      laser beam propagating through turbulent media
    journal: "Optical Engineering"
    volume: 40
    issue: 8
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      Origin of the gamma-gamma irradiance model used in
      aperturediv.channel. The plane-wave mapping from Rytov variance to the
      shape parameters is the widely reproduced form of this literature; this
      repository checks it for internal consistency against the model's own
      closed-form scintillation index and against sampled moments
      (validation/validate_channel_stats.py) rather than asserting a
      quotation.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Brennan
        given-names: "D. G."
    title: "Linear Diversity Combining Techniques"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IRE"
    volume: 47
    issue: 6
    year: 1959
    notes: >-
      Source for maximal-ratio, equal-gain and selection combining and for the
      optimality of maximal-ratio combining under perfect channel knowledge.
      The optimality statement is additionally enforced in this repository as
      a property-based test over random branch gains rather than taken on
      trust.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Zhu
        given-names: "X."
      - family-names: Kahn
        given-names: "J. M."
    title: "Free-space optical communication through atmospheric turbulence channels"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 50
    issue: 8
    year: 2002
    notes: >-
      Named in the README as prior art for lognormal-channel free-space
      optical link analysis. No numerical value in this repository is taken
      from it.
